# ============================================================
# flow/mst_phantom_strict.buda
#
# The same-bundle DOUBLE CHARGE, made DECISIVE.
#
# `CongestionPlanner::apply_segment` charges every segment
# independently, so two same-bundle segments whose metal
# COINCIDES charge the same band twice.  NUTS lets same-bundle
# segments share a track, so the books can say two buses where
# the metal is one.
#
# `tools/experiment/phantom_charge.py` measured that on the
# corpus and found it real but RARE: 4 coincident pairs in 555
# committed bundles (0.26% of segments), 3 bands affected, and
# the column that decides the question --
#
#     ... over capacity BECAUSE of it        0
#
# -- reading zero everywhere.  So the STRICT harm the mechanism
# implies does not occur on any design we route.  Rarity is the
# whole defence: cut 250 of big.buda already reads 91% full where
# its metal is at 50%, and one more co-placed duplicate on a
# tighter band is all it would take.
#
# THIS VEHICLE IS THAT ONE MORE.  It is an ADVERSARIAL
# CONSTRUCTION, not a design the corpus produces -- see WHAT IS
# FORCED below.  What it buys is a bound: the claim "no harm
# observed" becomes "no harm observed, and here is exactly how
# far a design has to be pushed before there is."
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/mst_phantom_strict.buda --no-viz
# Read: PYTHONPATH=build:src:tools python3 \
#           tools/experiment/phantom_charge.py flow/mst_phantom_strict.buda
#
# ── THE MECHANISM ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The four carrier blocks are `flow/mst_shared_leg_prefix.buda`'s,
# unchanged.  Both MST targets sit beyond hub's south-west
# CORNER, so `closest_points` returns that same corner for each
# edge and both leave it heading west:
#
#   seg1  H (2010,1010)->(1890,1010)   edge to `near`, len  120
#   seg3  H (2010,1010)->( 690,1010)   edge to `far`,  len 1320
#
# seg1 lies entirely inside seg3.  NUTS then places BOTH at track
# 964.5 -- one wire -- and the planner has charged it twice.
#
# ── WHAT IS FORCED, AND WHY ─────────────────────────────────
#
# THREE things, all because the shape is a structural loser and
# will not commit itself.  Everything else is ordinary.
#
# 1. THE PIN.  Unpinned, this bundle selects candidate 1
#    (TRUNK_H+MST@y900) and the duplicate rides candidates 18 and
#    19, which lose.  That is not luck -- it is the corpus-wide
#    finding (`base_rate_collinear.py`): in no bundle does any
#    candidate sharing the winner's CLASS carry redundancy, so a
#    duplicate almost always dies in a scoring overlay and never
#    reaches the committed field.  `select_topology 1 18` is what
#    overrules that.
#
# 2. THE BUS WIDTHS.  A phantom is one duplicate's charge, and it
#    is decisive only on a band already loaded to within that
#    charge of capacity.  The shared band here is 700 units (the
#    hub-top .. up-bottom gap, which the victim blocks span
#    EXACTLY so it stays one band).  Filling 700 units takes a
#    133-bit carrier and an 88-bit victim -- and the carrier must
#    be the WIDER of the two, because the planner commits widest
#    first and a phantom can only reach a bundle planned AFTER
#    the bundle carrying it.
#
#    The arithmetic bounds it.  Charge is 2.25*bits + 1 here, so
#    with the victim's demand D below the carrier's C (the
#    ordering constraint) and cap 700, harm needs
#    700 - 2C < D <= 700 - C with D < C, which forces C > 233 --
#    i.e. a carrier bus of at least ~104 bits.  There is no thin
#    carrier that does this.
#
# 3. BUNDLE 1 IS ALREADY IN BEST_EFFORT -- and this is the part
#    worth reading.  A carrier that wide no longer fits the
#    pinned candidate's own slide windows, so the planner emits
#
#      WARNING: Bundle 1: no candidate fits its slide windows
#      (bus width exceeds them); committing best-effort
#
#    and commits with overflow=100.118 of its own.  MEASURED, not
#    chosen: every (carrier, victim) pair that produces the harm
#    does this, and narrowing the gap band to 300 or 400 units to
#    allow a thinner carrier does not avoid it either (it just
#    loses the harm).  So on this geometry the phantom is
#    decisive only through a bundle the planner has ALREADY given
#    up on.  That is a real qualifier on the result and it points
#    the same way as everything else here.
#
# Point 2 is the structural content.  The knife-edge is not
# incidental: the phantom must be a significant fraction of a
# band's capacity, on a band wide enough to hold BOTH
# duplicates' charged perpendiculars (here they sit 275 units
# apart -- seg_perp 1235 and 1510 -- so no band narrower than
# that can carry the double charge at all).  Those two demands
# pull in opposite directions, which is the structural reason
# the corpus has no instance.
#
# ── THE RESULT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   phantom_charge.py, band (cut 6, band 6) on M4:
#     cap 700.00   usage 799.46   phantom 300.24
#     799.46 - 300.24 = 499.22 <= 700     the metal fits
#     799.46                    >  700     the books do not
#
#     ... over capacity                       1
#     ... over capacity BECAUSE of it         1     <-- was 0 everywhere
#
# and the planner says the rest itself:
#
#   WARNING: Bundle 2: no overflow-free candidate (even after
#   rip-up); committing least-cost candidate with overflow=118.46
#
# Bundle 2 has done nothing wrong and its own metal fits.  It is
# pushed out of the STRICT tier by demand that does not exist.
#
# THE CONTROL is `flow/mst_phantom_strict_control.buda` -- the
# same design with `set_trim_mst_legs on` -- where bundle 2's
# overflow is 0.  Read it as corroboration, NOT as the proof:
# the trim removes the duplicate METAL (bundle 1 goes 7 segments
# to 5), so the two arms are not "same wires, charged once".
# The attribution rests on the arithmetic above, which needs no
# second run: usage minus the phantom is under capacity.
#
# Bundle 1's own overflow (100.118) and its BEST_EFFORT warning
# are IDENTICAL in both arms -- a constant of the design, on a
# different band, not an effect of the duplicate.
# ============================================================

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33

corner_margin dx 10 dy 10

# ── carrier: flow/mst_shared_leg_prefix.buda's four blocks ──
# Both targets sit beyond hub's south-west corner, so the two MST
# edges start at one point and `near`'s short west leg lies
# inside `far`'s long one.
add_block hub   2000 1000  2100 1100
add_block near  1800  300  1900  400
add_block far    600  900   700  950

# Keeps hub--near and hub--far as DIRECT edges rather than a
# near--far chain:  hub--near 700, hub--far 1350, near--far 1600.
add_block up    2000 1800  2100 1900

# ── victim: spans the gap band EXACTLY ──────────────────────
# y 1100..1800 are already Hanan lines (hub top, up bottom), so
# these blocks add no new one and the gap stays a SINGLE 700-unit
# band -- wide enough to hold both charged perpendiculars (1235
# and 1510).  Split it and the double charge lands on two bands
# and nothing is decisive.
add_block vl   1200 1100  1300 1800
add_block vr   2300 1100  2400 1800

# Widest commits first, so the carrier must be the wider bus for
# its phantom to reach the victim at all.
add_bus d[133] hub.tx near.rx,far.rx,up.rx
add_bus v[88]  vl.tx vr.rx

run_bundler strict
generate_topologies

# Candidate 18 is MST_HV -- the shape carrying the duplicate.
# Unpinned it loses to candidate 1.  Verified by the vehicle's
# test, which asserts the pinned candidate's TYPE rather than
# trusting the index.
select_topology 1 18

run_planner 1
run_nuts

def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

run_detailed_nuts
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
